-- §6  What the fixtures decline to answer, which is the whole reason they exist.
#
# ⭐⭐ REPORTS ABOUT THE WORLD MUST NOT COUNT STIPULATIONS, AND THIS IS THE OTHER HALF OF THAT
# RULE. `rules.sql` composes reports/absences_in_the_corpus.sqlc and correctly does not compose
# this one: a fixture asserting independence on purpose would answer "has anybody tested the
# assumption?" with a yes nobody earned. But the fixture census is still worth reading -- it is
# just not a finding, so it belongs in an observation and not in a report of record.
#
# ⛔ THIS SECTION IS ITS CALLER, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE SECTION. A relation that composes,
# emits, and is read by nothing reads from the outside exactly like a relation that does not
# exist, which is what examples/observations/main.rs refuses to allow.
-- reports/absences_in_the_fixtures.sqlc, given the caller that makes it readable.
SELECT a.question AS "question!", a.reason::text AS "reason!", a.times AS "times!"
FROM (
    :compose(reports/absences_in_the_fixtures.sqlc)
) a
ORDER BY a.times DESC, a.question
